**`catalog/catalog.go:34`** — the body:

```go
names := make(map[string]string, len(skus))
asked := make(map[string]struct{}, len(skus)) // also holds the SKUs the source did not know
for _, sku := range skus {
	if _, ok := asked[sku]; ok {
		continue
	}
	asked[sku] = struct{}{}
	name, err := src.Get(sku)
	if errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound) {
		continue
	}
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("resolve %q: %w", sku, err)
	}
	names[sku] = name
}
return names, nil
```

Decisions worth naming:

- `asked` is separate from `names` because a SKU the source did not know is absent from the result but must still not cost a second round trip — `names` alone cannot express that.
- `fmt.Errorf("resolve %q: %w", …)` serves both audiences from one value: the log line shows the SKU and the underlying text, and `%w` keeps `ErrClosed` and any transport cause reachable through `errors.Is`/`errors.AsType`. No `catalog:` prefix in the wrap — the sentinels already carry it, so adding it would double up in the message.
- `errors.Is(err, ErrNotFound)` rather than `err == ErrNotFound`, since a Source may wrap it.

**`catalog/catalog_contract_test.go`** — one case per documented clause: ordering, unknown-SKU omission, repeated known SKU and repeated *unknown* SKU each costing one round trip, a transport failure abandoning the walk at the failing SKU, `ErrClosed` reachable via `errors.Is`, a wrapped cause still reachable, and empty input. Each case also asserts the exact round-trip sequence the fake source recorded, which is how the cost and abandonment clauses are checked.

